  "query_preview": "..Much less coverage of shower and thunderstorm activity \nat this time today compared to yesterday, just a few isolated \nshowers so far as of this writing. Heavy rainfall is still a \nconcern with any stronger storms that develop (mainly from \nSells eastward) later in the afternoon. Slight risk for \nexcessive rainfall continues for across southeast Arizona. \nOvernight into early tomorrow morning we could see some \nnocturnal convection as well, but confidence is lower in that \ncoming to fruition. However, if it does, heavy rainfall rates \ncontinue to be in the realm of possibility which is concerning \ngiven the amount of rain received over the past several days. A \nFlash Flood Watch goes into effect for all of southeast Arizona \nstarting at 11 PM MST this evening.\n\nBy mid morning tomorrow, we could see a break in any storm activity \nthat gets going before an uptick in both coverage and intensity \nduring the afternoon and evening hours as a disturbance moves north \nand east across southeast Arizona. The dynamics associated with this \nfeature will bring a better chance of more widespread heavy rainfall \nwith hourly rainfall rates of 1\"-2.5\", with some areas possibly \nseeing over 3\" of rain. Thus, the Flash Flood Watch continues until \n11 PM MST tomorrow evening. In coordination with surrounding offices \nand the Weather Prediction Center there is now a moderate risk for \nexcessive rainfall for portions of Pima, Pinal, and Graham counties \non Wednesday. If the disturbance moves along a bit faster, I\ncould see the heaviest rainfall axis shift south a bit into\ncentral/eastern Pima and Pinal counties. The strongest storms\nmay be capable of producing strong, gusty outflow winds and\nsmall hail, but this is still secondary to the elevated flash \nflood potential given the environment.\n\nThursday: Precipitable water remains elevated for this time of year \nwith a slight risk for excessive rainfall continuing east and south \nof Tucson. Showers and storms that develop will still have the \npotential for locally heavy rainfall, but without the disturbance \nnearby there will be a lack of forcing to help aid in higher \nrainfall rates like Wednesday.\n\nFriday through Monday: Drier air finally begins to filter into \nsoutheast Arizona as flow aloft shifts more westerly/southwesterly. \nThis could help limit thunderstorm activity somewhat and keep \nactivity mainly east and south of Tucson. The drier air will help \nlessen the threat for flash flooding, but brief heavy rainfall and \ngusty outflow winds will still accompany any stronger storms.\n\n8 to 14 day outlooks (Tue Sep 22 to Mon Sep 28)\nTemperature: Leaning above normal, 37-39% chance\nPrecipitation: Leaning above normal, 40-44% chance",
